From bd6cfdef8f40d911edbf3fc9830242dba3ee5be6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: permissionBRICK <40219477+permissionBRICK@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 01:41:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(face): low light on the colour camera: the IR camera
 decides alone

In an ordinary evening room the AI anti-spoofing model took real faces
for spoofs and the colour camera's recognition scored the user far
lower, while the IR camera, which lights the face itself, recognised as
in daylight. Measured on the ASUS Vivobook S 15 (both cameras' mode,
MiniFASNet V2 at 0.8): the colour picture's face box had a mean luma of
47-48 of 255 (the whole picture 55-59); MiniFASNet V2 scored the real
face 0.86-0.99 (a spoof), colour recognition 0.45-0.70, IR recognition
typically 0.75-0.83. In bright light: luma 86, spoof scores 0.01-0.08,
colour 0.75-0.80, IR 0.72-0.76. Face detection on the colour camera
still worked in the low light.

anti_spoofing.low_light_luma (default 70, 0 = off) and
recognition.ir_low_light_threshold (default 0.7): with a colour main
camera and a separate IR camera (ir_recognition, or the IR presence
check with faces in faces-ir/), the mean luma of the colour picture's
face box (or of the whole picture when it shows no face) is measured
first. Below the limit the IR camera decides alone, as Windows Hello
does: no AI anti-spoofing, no colour recognition; the IR face must
match faces-ir/ above the low-light threshold, which is never below the
IR camera's own threshold. When the colour camera saw a face, the IR
camera's face must be that face (the one-face check) at that moment
(kBothCamerasMaxGap); when it saw none in a dark picture (a dark room,
or the lens covered), the IR camera decides alone. A face on the colour
camera that is too small or cut off still compares nothing. An attempt
that starts on the IR camera (left open by the last one) matched its
face at the IR threshold while the colour camera opened; a dark colour
picture then compares it again at the low-light threshold, if that is
higher. With enough light, or the limit at 0, nothing changes.

The decision is a pure function (face/common/low_light.h). Each attempt
logs the light against the limit while the mode is on ("Low light on
the colour camera | face luma 48 < 70: the IR camera decides alone
(threshold 0.70)", "Enough light on the colour camera | face luma 86
(low light below 70)") and a match "Recognised on the IR camera alone
(low light)". The gap and the one-face checks moved into helpers, with
their log lines unchanged. A value out of range (luma outside 0-255, a
threshold outside 0-1, NaN) turns face off, as the other limits do.
Tests: low_light (new), face_both_cameras (fake frames with a grey
level, the threshold of each match), config_validation, config_golden.
